# Tutorial 08 — Character Movement and Easing
# [move], easing curves, @position, and timing movement with dialogue.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_08_movement
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[set ui.textBoxAsset none]
[set ui.continueIndicator spinning_arrow]
[if tutorial_still]
[set ui.reduceMotion true]
[else]
[set ui.reduceMotion false]
[endif]
[if !tutorial_initialized]
[set tutorial_count 0]
[flag tutorial_initialized]
[endif]
[set ui.characterHeightFactor 1.5]
[set ui.characterBaselineY 1.5]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect none]
{{BG_START}}
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender far_left happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: I'm on the far left. Now watch—
[move lavender center easeInOut 600]
[wait 600]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: That was {b}[move lavender center easeInOut 600]{/b}. One command, smooth animation.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
> [move] command syntax -> section_move
> Easing curves explained -> section_easing
> @position — custom named positions -> section_custom_pos
> Timing movement with dialogue -> section_move_dialogue
> Quick reference: movement patterns -> quick_reference
> Practice: live movement demos -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Quick Reference ─────────────────────────────────────────────

@label quick_reference
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Movement is blocking plus timing:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[move lavender center easeInOut 450]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[move lavender at 0.25,0.90 easeOut 600]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@position doorway 0.18 0.92{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show lavender doorway talking]{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Checklist: move for story reasons, pick easing that matches emotion, and wait long enough for the player to see it.
> Start the lesson sections -> section_move
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── [move] ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_move
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: {b}[move id position easing duration]{/b} — slides a character to a new position over time.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[move lavender center easeInOut 600]{/color}
narrator: {b}id{/b} — character to move (lavender).
narrator: {b}position{/b} — destination (far_left, left, center, right, far_right, or at X,Y).
narrator: {b}easing{/b} — acceleration curve (linear, easeIn, easeOut, easeInOut).
narrator: {b}duration{/b} — time in milliseconds. 300–800ms is typical.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Unlike [show] which teleports instantly, [move] animates over time. The player watches the character glide.
Lavender: You can also move to exact coordinates:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[move lavender at 0.30,0.80 easeInOut 500]{/color}
narrator: Moves to 30% from left, 80% from top.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Use [wait] after [move] to hold until the animation finishes before the next line:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[move lavender center easeInOut 600]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[wait 600]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: I'm here now.{/color}
> Next: easing curves -> section_easing
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Easing Curves ───────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_easing
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: The easing parameter controls {i}how{/i} the speed changes during the move. Four options:
narrator: {b}linear{/b} — constant speed. Mechanical. Use for sliding doors, conveyor belts.
narrator: {b}easeIn{/b} — starts slow, accelerates. Feels like pulling away, building momentum.
narrator: {b}easeOut{/b} — starts fast, decelerates. Feels like arriving, braking to a stop.
narrator: {b}easeInOut{/b} — slow start, fast middle, slow stop. The most natural. Use for most character movement.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Let me show you each one. Same distance, same duration, different feel:
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: {b}easeInOut{/b} — natural walk:
[move lavender right easeInOut 700]
[wait 700]
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: {b}easeIn{/b} — reluctant departure:
[move lavender right easeIn 700]
[wait 700]
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: {b}easeOut{/b} — decisive arrival:
[move lavender right easeOut 700]
[wait 700]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Same move, three different emotions. The easing is the body language.
> Next: custom positions -> section_custom_pos
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── @position ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_custom_pos
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: {b}@position{/b} defines a reusable named position with exact coordinates:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@position doorway 0.85 0.82{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@position window_seat 0.25 0.78{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@position stage_front 0.50 0.92{/color}
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Then use it anywhere you'd use a named position:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show lavender doorway talking]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[move lavender window_seat easeInOut 500]{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: "doorway" is more readable than "at 0.85,0.82". Define positions for spots characters visit often.
Lavender: Use inline {b}at X,Y{/b} for one-off placements. Use @position for recurring locations.
> Next: timing with dialogue -> section_move_dialogue
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Timing with Dialogue ───────────────────────────────────────

@label section_move_dialogue
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Two patterns for combining movement and speech:
narrator: {b}Pattern 1: Move, then speak{/b} — character arrives before talking:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[move lavender center easeInOut 400]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[wait 400]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: Okay, I'm here.{/color}
narrator: The [wait] matches the duration so dialogue appears after the move completes.
[show lavender center talking]
narrator: {b}Pattern 2: Speak while moving{/b} — skip the [wait]:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[move lavender right easeInOut 800]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: I'll be right over there if you need me.{/color}
narrator: The text appears immediately while the character is still sliding. Feels dynamic.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Pattern 1 for deliberate arrivals. Pattern 2 for casual movement and energy.
> Practice: live demos -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Three movement scenarios. Each one shows a different emotional use of [move].

@label practice_demos
Lavender: {b}Scene 1: Approach{/b}
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: Hey... can I talk to you for a second?
[move lavender center easeInOut 600]
[wait 600]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: {color=#8888cc}[move lavender center easeInOut 600]{/color} — closing distance. Reads as intention.
[wait 400]

Lavender: {b}Scene 2: Retreat{/b}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: I... I need some space.
[move lavender far_right easeIn 500]
[wait 500]
[show lavender far_right talking]
Lavender: {color=#8888cc}[move lavender far_right easeIn 500]{/color} — easeIn starts slow, speeds up. Reluctant, then committed.
[wait 400]

Lavender: {b}Scene 3: Dramatic entrance{/b}
[hide lavender]
[wait 500]
narrator: The room was quiet. Then — footsteps.
[show lavender far_right talking]
[move lavender center easeOut 500]
[wait 500]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: {color=#8888cc}[hide] + [show far_right] + [move center easeOut 500]{/color} — easeOut bursts in and slows to a stop.

[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Three emotions from the same command. Change only the easing or duration and the scene transforms.
> Replay demos -> practice_demos
